## 1. Fixed physical quantities and the fault contract

Let the old register contain fixed \(q\) qubits with its literal Hamiltonian \(K\geq0\). Let \(A\) contain any finite number \(m_A\) of reservoir and ancillary qubits; all their outputs remain part of the system. A reference \(R\) is arbitrary and untouched. Define

\[
S=\sum_{j\in S_{\rm sites}}n_j,\quad
B=\sum_{j\in A}n_j,\quad N=S+B,
\quad H_*=\Delta S,\quad F_0=K+\Delta B,\quad\Delta>0.
\tag{1}
\]

The symbol \(S\) in formulas denotes the old number operator; \(S_{\rm sites}\) denotes its sites. Keep \(\hbar\) and all physical energy units. This specified \(F_0\) is the original endpoint baseline. It is not the plateau surrogate.

Choose declared laboratory times \(0<t_a<t_b<T\). On the two ramp intervals \(I_+=[0,t_a]\) and \(I_-=[t_b,T]\), the nominal Hamiltonian is disjoint:

\[
H_0(t)=H_{S,\pm}(t)+\Delta B.
\tag{2}
\]

The old operators are the actual time-ordered positive-complement ramps of the parent. Their propagators are \(R_+\) and \(R_-\). In particular the turn-off map is forward evolution, not an inverse. On the plateau \(I_p=[t_a,t_b]\), require the parent's exact nominal property

\[
[H_0(t),N]=0.
\tag{3}
\]

This includes finite nominal compiled pulses, free scalar drift, all paid scalar reserves and final number-phase correction. Their exact phases and dressed target remain the parent's; this work theorem does not replace the complete-output comparator.

The actual closed-system Hamiltonian is

\[
H(t)=H_0(t)+E(t),\qquad E(t)=\sum_X E_X(t),
\tag{4}
\]

with finite Hermitian, integrable residuals supported on their actual site sets \(X\). Piecewise continuously differentiable controls suffice; energy-work formulas below include finite jumps if present. The literal term \(K\) is retained in \(H_0\); \(E\) records discrepancies of the additional controls or explicitly specified transient perturbations. It is not a redefinition of the old generator. A permanently mismatched installed field is an endpoint residual unless it is included in the actually specified baseline from the outset.

For local estimates supply nonnegative integrable bounds \(b_X(t)\) and real scalar functions \(c_X(t)\) such that

\[
\|E_X(t)-c_X(t)I_X\|\leq b_X(t).
\tag{5}
\]

Taking \(c_X=0\), \(b_X=\|E_X\|\) always suffices. For a Hermitian term its half spectral width is the best such bound. Subtracting a scalar in (5) is only a commutator/density-error estimate: the actual scalar, its control load, phase and endpoint energy are never removed from the physical model.

The theorem requires no random-noise average and allows arbitrary coherent correlations between residual terms. Actual positive-control realizability, support, incident strength and slew remain separately supplied certificates. If \(a_X(t)\geq\|E_X(t)\|\) and \(s_X(t)\geq\|\dot E_X(t)\|\), then safe incident bounds are

\[
J_{\rm actual}(v)\leq J_0(v)+\sum_{X\ni v}a_X(t),\qquad
S_{\rm actual}(v)\leq S_0(v)+\sum_{X\ni v}s_X(t).
\tag{6}
\]

Positivity does not follow from a small norm error and must be checked for the actual supplied controls. The complement loads already paid in the parent are part of \(J_0\). For onsite/edge residuals on a nearest-neighbor graph, \(|X|\leq2\); for higher-arity supplied old complements, use their actual fixed arity. No support splitting removes a physical incident load.

