## 2. Exact balance with physical charge leakage

Let \(\sigma(t)\) be the actual state on \(S A R\), with arbitrary initial correlations. Its old marginals at \(0,t_a,t_b,T\) are \(\sigma_0,\sigma_a,\sigma_b,\sigma_f\). Define the signed dimensionless transfers

\[
z_+=\frac{i}{\hbar}\int_{I_+}\operatorname{Tr}\sigma(t)[E(t),B]dt,
\quad
z_p=\frac{i}{\hbar}\int_{I_p}\operatorname{Tr}\sigma(t)[E(t),N]dt,
\quad
z_-=\frac{i}{\hbar}\int_{I_-}\operatorname{Tr}\sigma(t)[E(t),B]dt.
\tag{7}
\]

These are real, since \(i[E,Q]\) is Hermitian. On a ramp \([H_0,B]=0\), and on the plateau \([H_0,N]=0\). The Heisenberg expectation identity thus gives

\[
\langle B\rangle_a-\langle B\rangle_0=z_+,
\quad
\langle B\rangle_b-\langle B\rangle_a
  =z_p-\operatorname{Tr}S(\sigma_b-\sigma_a),
\quad
\langle B\rangle_f-\langle B\rangle_b=z_-.
\]

Therefore, with \(\Xi=\Delta(z_++z_p+z_-)\),

\[
\boxed{\;
W_{F_0}:=\langle F_0\rangle_f-\langle F_0\rangle_0
=\operatorname{Tr}K(\sigma_f-\sigma_0)
 -\operatorname{Tr}H_*(\sigma_b-\sigma_a)+\Xi.
\;}
\tag{8}
\]

This identity uses actual intermediate marginals, not ideal inputs. It is valid with entanglement, an initially correlated reservoir, coherent faults and arbitrary references. Taking marginals in its proof does not discard or reset any physical output. It does not yet identify \(W_{F_0}\) with actual prescribed-drive work when endpoint residuals or dissipation are present.

## 3. Work comparison from actual old errors and ramp errors

Let \(\rho\) be the declared old input and \(V\) the parent's target. Write \(\rho_V=V\rho V^*\). The nominal target work functional is exactly

\[
W_V=\operatorname{Tr}K(\rho_V-\rho)
 -\operatorname{Tr}H_*\big(R_-^*\rho_VR_--R_+\rho R_+^*\big).
\tag{9}
\]

Define unhalved trace errors

\[
\delta_0=\|\sigma_0-\rho\|_1,\qquad
\delta_f=\|\sigma_f-\rho_V\|_1,
\]

\[
e_+=\|\sigma_a-R_+\sigma_0R_+^*\|_1,\qquad
e_-=\|\sigma_f-R_-\sigma_bR_-^*\|_1.
\tag{10}
\]

These are properties or certified bounds of the actual protocol, not requests to measure an unknown input. They are meaningful for every allowed initial/reference state. Small old-marginal errors suffice for this work estimate; they do not certify correct old/reference correlations or full resource output. Those retain the parent's separate complete-isometry requirement. Triangle inequality and unitary invariance give

\[
\|\sigma_a-R_+\rho R_+^*\|_1\leq e_++\delta_0,
\quad
\|\sigma_b-R_-^*\rho_VR_-\|_1\leq e_-+\delta_f.
\]

For a Hermitian observable \(O\) and trace-zero Hermitian \(D\),
\(|\operatorname{Tr}OD|\leq\operatorname{width}(O)\|D\|_1/2\), by subtracting the midpoint of the spectral interval. Let \(\omega_K=\operatorname{width}(K)\) and \(\omega_*=q\Delta\). Subtracting (9) from (8) proves

\[
\boxed{\;
|W_{F_0}-W_V|
\leq\frac{\omega_K+q\Delta}{2}(\delta_0+\delta_f)
  +\frac{q\Delta}{2}(e_++e_-)+|\Xi|.
\;}
\tag{11}
\]

No reservoir width or dimension appears in these coefficients. The new task is to bound \(\Xi\) and \(e_\pm\) from a concrete fault contract; their actual size must not be presumed uniform.

An alternative observable calculation checks both the signs and coefficients. Put \(O_f=K-R_-H_*R_-^*\), \(O_0=K-R_+^*H_*R_+\), \(A_+=\sigma_a-R_+\sigma_0R_+^*\), and \(A_-=\sigma_f-R_-\sigma_bR_-^*\). Direct substitution in (8) gives

\[
W_{F_0}-W_V=\operatorname{Tr}O_f(\sigma_f-\rho_V)
-\operatorname{Tr}O_0(\sigma_0-\rho)
+\operatorname{Tr}(R_-H_*R_-^*)A_-
+\operatorname{Tr}H_*A_++\Xi.
\tag{11a}
\]

Each difference has trace zero, \(\operatorname{width}(O_j)\leq\omega_K+q\Delta\), and \(\|A_\pm\|_1=e_\pm\), again yielding (11).

### Old ramp disturbances need only old-touching residuals

During a ramp split \(E=E_A+E_{\rm touch}\), where \(E_A\) sums terms with \(X\cap S_{\rm sites}=\varnothing\). Use the comparison generator \(H_{S,\pm}(t)+\Delta B+E_A(t)\). Its propagator is \(R_\pm\otimes U_A\), so its old marginal is exactly \(R_\pm\sigma R_\pm^*\) even for correlated input. Unitary Duhamel and partial-trace contraction yield

\[
e_\pm\leq\min\{2,2\eta_{S,\pm}\},\qquad
\eta_{S,\pm}=\frac1\hbar\int_{I_\pm}
             \sum_{X:X\cap S_{\rm sites}\ne\varnothing}b_X(t)dt.
\tag{12}
\]

The scalar sums in (5) only change a global comparison phase and do not affect density matrices. A global bound \(g(t)\geq\inf_c\|E(t)-cI\|\) instead gives \(e_\pm\leq\min\{2,2\eta_\pm\}\), \(\eta_\pm=\hbar^{-1}\int_{I_\pm}g(t)dt\). Either valid estimate can be used. If old-site incident residual bounds obey \(\sum_{X\ni j}b_X(t)\leq b_S(t)\), (12) gives \(\eta_{S,\pm}\leq q\hbar^{-1}\int_{I_\pm}b_S(t)dt\), independently of \(m_A\). This counts each old-touching term at least once; it makes no claim that the corresponding sum over all reservoir sites is bounded.

## 4. Local charge certificates with explicit support dependence

For either number operator \(Q=B\) or \(Q=N\), define its charged sites \(T_Q\) and \(r_X^Q=|X\cap T_Q|\). Only \(Q_X=\sum_{j\in X\cap T_Q}n_j\) enters \([E_X,Q]\). Since \(Q_X\) has spectrum in \([0,r_X^Q]\),

\[
\|[E_X,Q]\|
=\|[E_X-c_XI,Q_X-r_X^QI/2]\|
\leq r_X^Q b_X.
\tag{13}
\]

Thus a supplied sharper certificate \(a_X^Q(t)\geq\|i[E_X(t),Q]\|\), including the exact value zero for a structurally conserving term, can replace \(r_X^Qb_X\). One bound is

\[
|\Xi|\leq\Lambda_{\rm loc}:=\frac{\Delta}{\hbar}
\left[\int_{I_+\cup I_-}\sum_X a_X^B(t)dt
      +\int_{I_p}\sum_X a_X^N(t)dt\right].
\tag{14}
\]

The coefficient in (13) is one, not two times the support size; the charge centering is essential to that improvement. It is sharp: on \(r\) charged qubits, \(E=b(|0^r\rangle\langle1^r|+|1^r\rangle\langle0^r|)\) has norm \(b\) and commutator norm \(rb\). For a nearest-neighbor edge this already realizes the \(r=2\) factor.

In particular, \(N\)-conserving plateau residuals have zero leakage even if their number or total norm is extensive. Examples include amplitude/phase errors in exchange terms \(z\sigma_i^+\sigma_j^-+\bar z\sigma_i^-\sigma_j^+\), diagonal number fields, diagonal CZ generators, and a full SWAP generator between the designated qubits. This is charge conservation, not conservation of an incorrectly substituted physical energy baseline. On a ramp old-only errors have zero \(B\)-leakage but can produce the old ramp errors in (12). Number-changing resource errors need their nonzero certificates. Exact conservation is recognized from a supplied structural expression or certificate; the theorem does not provide an equality oracle for arbitrary computable matrix entries.

### A separate global-norm certificate without a reservoir-width factor

Suppose a residual \(E(t)\) has charge bandwidth at most the integer \(r_Q\geq0\): its matrix elements between \(Q\)-eigenspaces vanish when their integer eigenvalues differ by more than \(r_Q\). A sum of terms supported on at most \(r\) qubits has this property with \(r_Q\leq r\), regardless of the number of summands or graph size. The family conclusion requires a common finite \(r\) independent of reservoir size. Define its finite charge-Fourier components by

\[
E_d=\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}
     e^{i\theta Q}E e^{-i\theta Q}e^{-id\theta}d\theta.
\tag{15}
\]

For \(d\ne0\), scalar subtraction gives \(\|E_d\|\leq\inf_c\|E-cI\|\leq g\). Also \([Q,E_d]=dE_d\), and only \(-r_Q\leq d\leq r_Q\) occur. Therefore the elementary finite sum gives

\[
\boxed{\ \|[E,Q]\|\leq r_Q(r_Q+1)g.\ }
\tag{16}
\]

This constant is deliberately conservative; no optimal operator Bernstein inequality is imported. The proof uses only finite integer-charge decomposition and unitary norm invariance. It is valid for noncommuting residual terms and does not require simultaneous diagonalization of \(E\) or \(K\).

With a common bandwidth bound \(r\), and \(\eta_j=\hbar^{-1}\int_{I_j}g(t)dt\),

\[
|\Xi|\leq\Lambda_{\rm glob}:=
\Delta r(r+1)(\eta_++\eta_p+\eta_-).
\tag{17}
\]

The stronger available estimate among (14), (17), or their pointwise minimum can be used. A global norm is not the sum of the local residual norms: \(\inf_c\|E-cI\|\leq\sum_X b_X\), so choosing \(g=\sum_Xb_X\) is sufficient, potentially loose. A separately certified global bound may be tighter. Equation (16) is what permits a supplied small global norm to give a width-independent charge estimate under bounded bandwidth. Without that structural hypothesis, the trivial bound involves the full charge width instead.

Combining (11), (12) with its global alternative, and (17) proves the main directly usable corollary:

\[
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
|W_{F_0}-W_V|\leq{}
 &\frac{\omega_K+q\Delta}{2}(\delta_0+\delta_f)\\
 &+q\Delta(\eta_++\eta_-)
  +\Delta r(r+1)(\eta_++\eta_p+\eta_-).
\end{aligned}}\tag{18}
\]

For genuinely onsite/nearest-neighbor residuals \(r=2\) suffices, so the last coefficient is \(6\Delta\), and the ramp coefficient is \((q+6)\Delta\). None depends on the reservoir length. The result is conditional on the residual norms and fixed support/bandwidth; it is not an estimate from final trace error alone.

