## 6. Endpoint controls and actual driven work

For closed coherent dynamics with a differentiable prescribed Hamiltonian,

\[
W_{\rm drive}=\int_0^T\operatorname{Tr}\sigma(t)\dot H(t)dt
 =\operatorname{Tr}H(T)\sigma(T)-\operatorname{Tr}H(0)\sigma(0),
\tag{22}
\]

with the corresponding jump-work terms for piecewise controls. If \(H(0)=F_0+D_0\), \(H(T)=F_0+D_f\), then

\[
W_{\rm drive}=W_{F_0}
 +\operatorname{Tr}D_f\sigma(T)-\operatorname{Tr}D_0\sigma(0).
\tag{23}
\]

Therefore every bound above becomes a bound on \(|W_{\rm drive}-W_V|\) after adding the absolute value of that actual endpoint term, or the safe allowance \(\|D_f\|+\|D_0\|\). For local endpoint decompositions, summing their actual norms is sufficient. No trace-zero centering removes their scalar parts because the operators differ at the two endpoints. Exact restoration \(D_0=D_f=0\) makes \(W_{F_0}=W_{\rm drive}\).

A field detuning \(\sum_j\delta_jn_j\) conserves number but, if left installed at the endpoints, is a real \(D\). It cannot be silently absorbed into an exactly equal-gap baseline. Similarly, a positive scalar switched from zero to \(cI\) changes prescribed-drive work by \(c\) while changing no density matrix or charge. This exact one-term example disproves dropping (23).

For absolute rather than net work, add the following safe residual allowance to the nominal norm-variation bound \(\int\|\dot H_0(t)\|dt\):

\[
\int_0^T\!\|\dot E(t)\|dt\leq\sum_X\int_0^T\!\|\dot E_X(t)\|dt,
\tag{24}
\]

plus jumps. A small integrated amplitude does not control this variation. Constant incident strength/slew certificates such as (6), and the actual count of simultaneously driven sites, must be retained if an absolute-work bound is requested. The present fixed coefficients for net work do not imply constant total control effort.

## 7. Dissipation is a different energy ledger

The theorem above is for coherent Hamiltonian faults. It does not model loss, measurement, reset or a bath by an effective Hermitian residual. If a separately supplied open-system model is

\[
\dot\sigma=-(i/\hbar)[H,\sigma]+\mathcal D_t(\sigma),
\]

then charge balances acquire \(\operatorname{Tr}\sigma\mathcal D_t^*(Q)\) in addition to the Hamiltonian terms in (7). For a specified local jump term \(\mathcal D_X(\sigma)=\gamma_X(L_X\sigma L_X^*-\{L_X^*L_X,\sigma\}/2)\) with nonnegative rate \(\gamma_X\), direct expansion gives

\[
\mathcal D_X^*(Q)
 =\tfrac{\gamma_X}{2}\big(L_X^*[Q,L_X]+[L_X^*,Q]L_X\big),
\quad
\|\mathcal D_X^*(Q)\|\leq\gamma_X r_X^Q\|L_X\|^2.
\tag{25}
\]

The last bound follows from charge centering as in (13). This is a conditional algebraic observation for the supplied model, not a derivation or supplier of a dissipator. It can bound baseline energy transfer, but actual prescribed-drive work now also needs the heat term

\[
Q_{\rm bath}=\int_0^T\operatorname{Tr}H(t)\mathcal D_t(\sigma(t))dt,
\quad
W_{\rm drive}=W_{F_0}+\text{endpoint term}-Q_{\rm bath}.
\tag{26}
\]

For example, a single qubit with fixed Hamiltonian \(\Delta n\), no drive, initial excited state and a supplied decay law \(p(t)=e^{-\gamma t}\) has \(W_{F_0}=\Delta(e^{-\gamma T}-1)\), while \(W_{\rm drive}=0\); the difference is heat. A Hamiltonian-norm certificate cannot pay this missing term. Equation (11) remains an algebraic comparison if the actual old ramp errors and all extra charge sources are supplied, but (12), (18) and the drive interpretation cannot be imported unchanged into an unspecified open-system model.

Calling the generator energy-flow term in (26) physical bath heat requires the supplied open-system model and its energy identification. The algebraic identity does not construct or identify a microscopic bath.

## 8. Exact counterexamples to stronger replacements

### 8.1 Small full trace error alone does not control energy uniformly

On \(m\) resource qubits compare \(|0^m\rangle\) with
\(|\psi\rangle=\sqrt{1-p}|0^m\rangle+\sqrt p|1^m\rangle\). The unhalved trace distance of their pure-state density matrices is \(2\sqrt p\), while their \(\Delta B\) means differ by \(m\Delta p\). With fixed trace error \(\varepsilon=2\sqrt p\), the discrepancy is \(m\Delta\varepsilon^2/4\). Tensoring an unchanged old/reference state leaves all old errors zero. This refutes a universal work/energy replacement by trace error alone; it says nothing against (18), whose physical residual/bandwidth budget has not been supplied for these states.

### 8.2 Constant local fault strength can give extensive leakage on the actual sine packet

This example uses the parent's real prefix packet, not an unmentioned vacuum replacement. On a branch with \(L\geq7\), \(M\geq L\) (in particular the parent's \(M=L+q\)), let

\[
|\beta\rangle=\sqrt{\frac2{L+1}}\sum_{\ell=1}^L
 \sin\frac{\pi\ell}{L+1}|1^\ell0^{M-\ell}\rangle.
\]

Set \(n=L+1\), \(J=\{\lceil7n/8\rceil,\ldots,L\}\), so \(s=|J|=\lfloor n/8\rfloor\). For \(j\in J\), its occupation \(p_j\) obeys

\[
p_j=\frac2n\sum_{\ell=j}^L\sin^2(\pi\ell/n)
\leq\frac2n(L-j+1)\leq\frac14.
\tag{27}
\]

The packet is real in the number basis, so \(\langle Y_j\rangle=0\). During a nominal conserving idle plateau choose a smooth nonnegative envelope \(\dot\theta(t)\) of total area \(\theta\in(0,\pi/2)\), zero at both ends, and resource-only positive onsite residuals

\[
E_j(t)=\hbar\dot\theta(t)\left[I+
 e^{-i\Delta t n_j/\hbar}X_j e^{i\Delta t n_j/\hbar}\right],
\quad j\in J.
\tag{28}
\]

An absolute-time phase shift accounts for any preceding resource drift; equivalently begin this formula in the known interaction-picture coordinate of the packet. Place the envelope inside an idle interval and end it before the final number-phase slot. This is an explicit coherent fault example, not a claim that the required reference is native. Its onsite blocks are positive, have norm \(2\hbar\dot\theta\), and obey \(\|\dot E_j\|\leq2\hbar|\ddot\theta|+\Delta|\dot\theta|\). A fixed smooth envelope therefore gives common finite amplitude/slew bounds independent of \(L\); it can fit any specified positive headroom by choosing its fixed duration and amplitude accordingly. All residuals vanish at the endpoints. In the interaction picture they implement, with an explicit scalar phase \(e^{-is\theta}\), the product \(\prod_{j\in J}e^{-i\theta X_j}\). Hence

\[
\langle B\rangle_f-\langle B\rangle_0
 =\sin^2\theta\sum_{j\in J}(1-2p_j)
 \geq\tfrac{s}{2}\sin^2\theta.
\tag{29}
\]

Choose a nominal semantic identity word so the old state is unaffected by these resource-only faults; its dressed old target includes the fixed nominal ramps. Then \(\delta_0=\delta_f=e_+=e_-=0\), while actual extra same-baseline work is at least \(s\Delta\sin^2\theta/2\), growing with \(L\). The retained full resource output is changed, not erased. This refutes replacing (14) or (19) by a constant per-site allowance with no count. It is consistent with both bounds: the local leakage allowance is \(s\Delta\theta\), and the global centered residual action is \(s\theta\). Neither is uniformly small. Exact number-conserving faults, a fixed number of faulty sites, or an appropriately shrinking summed residual remain valid alternatives.

### 8.3 Bounded bandwidth is additional information

The \(r\)-qubit operator following (13) changes charge by \(r\) and saturates \(\|[E,Q]\|=r\|E\|\). Allowing its support/charge bandwidth to grow with the reservoir invalidates a fixed coefficient inferred from global norm alone. Conversely, many local terms may have a small *certified global sum* through cancellations, and (16) remains valid for that sum with fixed bandwidth. The theorem does not assume either worst-case summation or cancellation without the corresponding certificate.

These are exact counterexamples to specified false substitutions, not a general no-go for local accuracy families. The positive alternatives are (14), (17), exact charge conservation, fixed faulty support, and structurally controlled dissipative energy transfer with an explicit heat ledger. No route failure is promoted to a universal impossibility claim.

